{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Late-horizon upside bias: from ~day 20 onward the model systematically underestimates strong rallies (misses upside tails). Apply an increasing positive adjustment with horizon during uptrends (e.g., +0.5 to +2.5 €/t from day 20→30 when momentum is positive).",
    "Mid-horizon downside overshoot: around ~days 12–20 the model tends to forecast too deep/too early pullbacks versus realized (excess mean-reversion). Damp negative deviations toward the recent spot/10d mean by ~30–50% in that window.",
    "Trend lag after regime shift: when the market breaks higher (as in the 70→75 move), the model reacts with a delay and keeps forecasts anchored near the prior range. Add a momentum/regime filter: if spot > prior 10d high (or >10d mean + 1σ), shift the whole forward curve upward and reduce mean-reversion strength.",
    "Over-smoothing of volatility: the model’s path is too flat; realized outcomes show larger swings and late acceleration. Increase forecast variance conditional on rising momentum (widen bands / allow steeper slope), especially beyond 2–3 weeks.",
    "Spike handling: after sharp upside moves, the model often predicts a partial retracement that is too large; in reality, consolidation occurs at higher levels. Cap predicted retracements after upside spikes (e.g., limit pullback to ≤40% of the last 5d gain unless momentum turns negative)."
  ]
}
